+    "debriefMsg": "\nIn this simulation you should learn to recognize the clinical presentation of hyperkalemia in the context of an expanding subdural hematoma.\n\nYou must treat the patient aggressively along 3 fronts.  From the standpoint of head injury, the patient must proceed emergently to surgical decompression.  If the patient had a non-life threatening surgical indication, you would medically stabilize before proceeding.  This scenario forces the issue and you will be penalized for waiting.  Secondly, you must recognize and treat hyperkalemia.  Insulin, albuterol, and bicarbonate should be administered.  Under most circumstances of hyperkalemia, glucose should be given with the insulin.  Intravenous calcium should be given in cases of arrhythmia.  Lastly, you must also begin treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis, the underlying cause of the hyperkalemia.  This principally involves volume resuscitation to replace the osmotic diuresis of hyperglycemia, and insulin therapy with a continuous infusion to reverse the metabolic derangements.  ICU therapy of DKA will often require potassium replacement, as the osmotic diuresis causes low total-body potassium, even in the face of elevated plasma levels.  This treatement is outside the scope of the simulation case, and is not addressed here.\n\n", 
