+    "learningObj": "\n20 year old female for decompressive craniotomy(68 kg)\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  List common clinical scenarios for hyperkalemia in anesthetic practice.\n2.  Know what drugs to avoid in the context of actual or possible hyperkalemia\n3.  Describe the management of hyperkalemia in anesthetic practice.\n4.  Describe the basic management of subdural hematoma - and the importance of avoiding increases in ICP.\n5.  A minor objective is to recall the management of DKA. \n\n During this scenario, you are faced with a complicated set of life-threatening conditions.  It is critically important to correctly prioritize these in terms of immediacy of threat to life.  In this case, the subdural hematoma represents the most immediate threat to life, and surgical evacuation must proceed without delay.  During management of this, you will also have to address hyperkalemia, most likely caused by diabetic ketoacidosis.  In this context, the hyperkalemia will ultimately resolve with treatment of DKA, however circumstances may require temporizing with acute therapies.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan:\n1.  Routine monitoring + arterial line for both frequent blood gases and possible BP lability with decompression\n2.  Evaluation and frequent monitoring and correction of the patient's electrolyte and ECG status\n3.  Preoxygenate with assisted breaths to begin hyperventilation.\n4.  Modified rapid sequence induction with narcotics, etomidate, and a nondepolarizing neuromuscular agent, gentle hyperventilation to prevent hypercarbia and resultant increase in ICP as well as extracellular potassium shift. \n5.  Tracheal intubation with cricoid pressure.\n6.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, relaxant, and inhaled agent.  Consider use of mannitol for neurosurgical purposes, however be aware that mannitol has been associated with hyperkalemia.\n7.  Treat ketoacidosis with volume resuscitation, insulin, and frequent monitoring of metabolic status, electrolytes, and blood glucose.\n8.  Continued hyperventilation until decompression.\n9.  Consider leaving the patient intubated for further ICU management of ketoacidosis and altered mental state.\n\nHyperkalemia\nHyperkalemia is simply an elevation in plasma potassium that can be caused by exogenous administration of potassium, shift of intracellular potassium into the extracellular space, and impairments in ability to eliminate potassium. \n\nClinical Points\n1.  Hyperkalemia may occur in a number of clinical scenarios - a full discussion of etiologies exceeds the scope of this exercise.\n2.  Renal insufficiency should always raise the concern for hyperkalemia\n3.  Acidosis, whether metabolic or respiratory, can cause shift of potassium from intracellular to extracellular.\nBecause the vast majority of total body potassium lies in the intracellular space, these shifts can cause large and rapid changes in plasma K concentrations.  From another point of view, the plasma K concentration is not necessarily reflective of total body potassium content.\n4.  Iatrogenic potassium sources can be obvious (IV KCl) or hidden (blood transfusions)\n5.  Succinylcholine - causes potassium release.  In patients with burns or neuromuscular disease, the magnitude of this release can be exaggerated and cause hyperkalemic cardiac arrest.  Prolonged immobility is also a risk factor for exaggerated response to succinylcholine. \n6.  Rhabdomyolysis and crush injury can cause massive release of potassium from dying muscle cells and also from acidosis. Similarly, tumor lysis syndrome following institution of anti-tumor therapies \n7.  Administration of mannitol has been associated with hyperkalemia through a mechanism which is still debated.  \n\nPresenting Symptoms of Hyperkalemia\nIn the awake patient, symptoms may be limited to weakness - even flaccid paralysis.\nCardiac conduction abnormalities may manifest in several ways:\n1.  Tall, peaked T-waves may be the first sign, often associated with QT shortening \n2.  As K+ increases, the PR interval and QRS duration increase\n3.  P-waves may disappear\n4.  Both the PR interval and QRS duration increase, progressing to the \"sinusoidal\" electrocardiogram.  \n5.  Other various AV conduction blocks, bundle branch blocks, and fascicular blocks may also occur. \n6.  Electrical silence or ventricular fibrillation may occur.\n\nTreatment of Hyperkalemia\n1.  Stop administration of potassium-containing solutions or medications.\n2.  In cases with arrhythmia, intravenous calcium has been demonstrated to be useful.  Calcium does not lower potassium levels, but appears to havea stabilizing effect.\n3.  Inhaled or intravenous beta-agonists can lower potassium via a extra-to-intracellular shift.\n4.  Intravenous insulin and glucose also causes intracellular shift of potassium.\n5.  Raising pH via intravenous bicarbonate or hyperventilation may help to shift K+ ions intracellular to balance the outflow of H+ ions shifting out.\n6.  Use of potassium-binding resins (kayexelate) has little utility in the short-term, but may be useful over a time scale of many hours.\n7.  Dialysis may be useful in severe cases\n8.  Remember that the majority of these interventions are transient in nature - shifting K+ ions intracellularly.  It is necessary to recheck frequently and repeat as appropriate.\n\nCase author: Christopher R. Lee, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: April 1, 2015\n\n", 
