+    "learningObj": "\n74 year old with aortic stenosis (88 kg)\n\nThis patient has moderately severe aortic stenosis, but denies symptoms except for one syncopal episode. \n\nLearning Objectives\n1. Describe the pathophysiology of aortic stenosis.\n2. List the general anesthetic considerations for aortic stenosis.\n\nPathophysiology of Aortic Stenosis\n1. The tight aortic valve obstructs flow from the left ventricle to the aorta creating a pressure gradient. \n2. Left ventricular compliance is reduced and filling pressure rises as the wall thickens.  \n3. Hypotension can lead to rapid spiral of ischemia, worsened hypotension, and further ischemia.\n\nClinical Presentation of Aortic Stenosis\n1. Angina - initial symptom in 50-70% of cases\n2. Syncope - initial symptom in 15-30% of cases, life expectancy 3-4 years\n3. Congestive heart failure - life expectancy 1-2 years\n\nEvaluation of Aortic Stenosis\n1. Mild - asymptomatic with physiologic compensatory left ventricular hypertrophy\n2. Moderate - symptomatic impairment with left ventricular dilation as well as hypertrophy; valve  area 0.7-0.9 sq cm\n3. Severe - increased LVEDP with decreased stroke  volume and increased left atrial pressures (25-30 mmHg) leading to pulmonary edema; valve area < 0.5 sq cm. Blood flow is not impeded until valve area falls below 0.5-0.7 sq cm.\n4. All asymptomatic patients with valve area <0.7 sq cm or an aortic valve gradient > 50 mmHg should have an elective aortic valve replacement since only 18% of these patients are alive five years after diagnosis.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations for Aortic Stenosis\n1. Maintain sinus rhythm if possible. Atrial kick is important - may contribute 40% of left ventricular filling.\n2. Avoid tachycardia - high heart rate aggravates ischemia and decreases filling.\n3. Maintain adequate preload, but avoid pulmonary edema\n4. Maintain high SVR for adequate perfusion pressure. Left ventricular afterload is fixed by the valve area.\n5. Avoid myocardial depression\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Premedicate to prevent tachycardia\n2. Routine monitoring plus direct arterial pressure. Consider PA catheter for LV filling pressure and SVR. However, there is an increased risk of arrhythmias with PA catheter.\n3. Administer fluid bolus to restore intravascular volume.\n4. Preoxygenate\n5. Moderate amount of narcotics, with roughly half MAC potent inhalation agent\n6. Maintain adequate preload\n7. Keep heart rate down and SVR up - use phenylephrine\n8. Treat hypotension immediately\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 19, 2013\n\n", 
