+    "leanBodyMass": 100, 
+    "learningObj": "\n53 year old obese male for colon resection (150 kg)\n\nThis patient presents a difficult set of problems: asthma and a potentially difficult intubation.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. Describe anesthetic management of asthma.\n2. Describe the management of a patient with asthma and potentially difficult airway.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1. Asthma\n2. Potentially difficult intubation\n\nFrom the physical exam, this patient has a potentially difficult intubation. Regional anesthesia would be difficult for this procedure. General anesthesia delivered by mask would avoid airway instrumentation but would be difficult and would not protect against aspiration. Laryngeal mask airway does not prevent aspiration either. The choice is whether to secure the airway prior to induction of anesthesia and risk triggering bronchospasm or intubate under deep general anesthesia and risk losing control of the airway and aspiration. While bronchospasm may be difficult, hypoxia is definitely to be avoided. Therefore, intubate prior to induction of general anesthesia if management of the airway is a major concern.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring plus direct arterial pressure\n2. Oxygen by mask\n3. Treat with inhaled beta-agonist\n4. Carefully sedate with benzodiazepines for patient cooperation.\n5. Very careful, gentle, and thorough topical anesthesia of the airway.\n6. Fiberoptic intubation\n7. Induce anesthesia with propofol \n8. Maintain deep anesthesia with narcotics, inhalation agent, and competitive neuromuscular blocker\n9. Extubate promptly after return of airway reflexes.\n\nDifferential Diagnosis of Bronchospasm - Not all Wheezing is Bronchospasm.\n1. Obstructed ETT - kinking, secretions, cuff\\\n2. Endobronchial intubation\n3. Bronchial asthma\n4. Bronchospasm due to inadequate anesthesia, airway manipulation, or predisposing medications such as anticholinesterase, tubocurarine, morphine, barbiturates\n5. Pulmonary edema\n6. Aspiration\n7. Pneumothorax\n\nTreatment of Bronchospasm\n1. Hand ventilate to rule out mechanical problem with ventilator\n2. Check anesthesia circuit and valves - if possible use ambu bag\n3. Check location of endotracheal tube - auscultate and confirm end-tidal CO2\n4. Pass suction catheter or fiberoptic scope down the endotracheal tube to rule out obstruction\n5. Inhaled beta-agonists\n6. Deepen anesthesia\n\nDifficult Airway\n1. The unconscious or paralyzed patient with a difficult airway will be hard to ventilate and intubate. It is best to anticipate this situtation by careful preoperative examination and secure the airway prior to induction of anesthesia.\n2. Management options for `awake' intubation include oral laryngoscopy, blind nasal, and fiberoptic intubation. Don't forget to use some sedatives and topical anesthesia.\n3. If you can't ventilate and can't intubate the patient, call for help immediately. Try to establish a clear airway with maneuvers such as neck extension, jaw lift, and oral and nasal airways. Try a couple different laryngoscope blades. If these attempts fail or if the patient desaturates, place a laryngeal mask airway or perform a cricothyroidotomy and use jet ventilation. Do not persist with direct laryngoscopy too long. If direct visualization proves very difficult cricothyroidotomy must be performed early.\n4. Once a laryngeal mask airway has been placed you may be able to insert an endotracheal tube through the LMA using fiberoptic  guidance.\n5. Alternatively, a retrograde intubation can be performed by passing a wire through the cricothyroid catheter up into the mouth. Run the wire into the suction port of a fiberoptic bronchoscope and guide the scope through the vocal cords. Remove the wire when the scope is in the trachea. Advance the scope until the carina is visualized and advance the endotracheal tube over the bronchoscope.\n6. The patient must be informed that a difficult airway was encountered and a letter should be placed in the medical record.\n\nReferences\n1. Wittels: Obstructive sleep apnea and obesity. Otolaryngol Clin North Am 1990; 23: 751\n2. Bishop. Bronchospasm: avoiding an anesthetic disaster. ASA Refresher Courses in Anesthesiology.\n3. Eames: Effect of etomidate, propofol, and thiopental on respiratory resistance. Anesthesiology 1996; 84: 1307.\n4. Rooke, Choi, Bishop: The effect of isoflurane, halothane, sevoflurane, and thiopental/nitrous oxide on respiratory system resistance after tracheal intubation. Anesthesiology 1997; 86(6):1294-9.\n5. Eames, Rooke, Wu, Bishop: Comparison of the effects of etomidate, propofol, and thiopental on respiratory resistance after tracheal intubation. Anesthesiology 1996; 84(6):1307-11.\n6. Bishop, Rooke: Sevoflurane for patients with asthma. Anesth Analg 2000; 91(1):245-6. \n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 19, 2013\n\n", 
