+    "durationSurgery": 1200, 
+    "helpFlag": true, 
+    "imageSet": 4, 
+    "initialSat": 82, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 80, 
+    "learningObj": "\n54 year old with cor pulmonale for emergency laparotomy (82 kg)\n\nThis patient has severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease which caused pulmonary artery hypertension and right ventricular failure.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Describe the pathophysiology of cor pulmonale.\n2.  List the goals of anesthetic management of patients with cor pulmonale.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations:\nCor pulmonale - right ventricular failure\nSevere pulmonary disease - V/Q mismatching, increased risk of bronchospasm and post-op respiratory failure\nFull stomach (blood) - prevention of aspiration\n\nManagement of Cor Pulmonale:\nThe goals of management of the patient with cor pulmonale are to decrease the work of the right ventricle and improve pulmonary function. Delay elective procedures until the patient's condition is optimal.\n\nReverse hypoxemia, hypercarbia, and acidosis\nTreat infections with antibiotics\nBronchodilators\nDigoxin and lasix\nReexpand the lungs, clear secretions\nCorrect fluid and electrolyte abnormalities\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan:\n1.  Avoid pre-operative medication\n2.  Arterial and PA catheters\n3.  Preoxygenate, watch for loss of hypoxic drive\n4.  Provide adequate anesthetic depth with narcotics, etomidate, midazolam, and lidocaine while applying cricoid pressure\n6.  Tracheal intubation, suction frequently\n7.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, and isoflurane\n8.  Extubate after return of airway reflexes\n9.  Monitor arterial blood gas, central venous pressure, pulmonary artery pressure, wedge pressure and cardiac output closely\n10.  Avoid hyperventilation which will cause alkalosis and hypokalemia\n11.  Respiratory drive may be extra sensitive to narcotics\n12.  Avoid conditions that increase pulmonary vascular resistance - hypoxia, hypercarbia, acidosis, hypothermia.\n13.  Milrinone is a good inotrope for cor pulmonale if there is not systemic hypotension.\n14.  Dobutamine, epinephrine or norepinephrine are good inotropes if systemic hypotension is present.\n15.  Sodium nitroprusside, nitroglycerin, milrinone, amlodipine, prostaglandin E1 and nitric oxide can be used to lower pulmonary vascular resistance.\n\nClinical Points:\nIn heart failure, the pulmonary artery systolic, diastolic and wedge (occlusion) pressures are all raised. Typically the wedge pressure is one or two mmHg lower than PA diastolic pressure.\nIn cor pulmonale, the pulmonary wedge pressure may be much lower than PA diastolic pressure due to high pulmonary vascular resistance.\n\nNotes:\nNitric oxide, epoprostenol and inhaled milrinone are not available in this version of the simulator.\nTry the older therapies instead for this simulated case.\n\nReferences:\n1.  Steudel, Hurford, Zapol: Inhaled nitric oxide: basic biology and clinical applications. Anesthesiology 1999; 91: 1090-1121\n2.  Hinderliter, Willis, Barst: Effects of long-term infusion of prostacyclin (epoprostenol) on echocardiographic measures of right ventricular structure and function in primary pulmonary hypertension. Circulation 1997; 95: 1479-1486\n3.  Haraldsson, Kieler-Jensen, Ricksten: The additive pulmonary vasodilatory effects of inhaled prostacylcin and inhaled milrinone in postcardiac surgical patients with pulmonary hypertension. Anesth Analg 2001; 93: 1439-1445\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 20, 2013\n\n", 
