+    "learningObj": "40 year old for excision of pheochromocytoma (80 kg)\n\nThis patient has a pheochromocytoma (pheo) and severe hypertension.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Describe the pathophysiology of pheochromocytomas.\n2.  Discuss pre-op management of a patient with pheo.\n3.  Discuss monitoring of patient during resection of pheo.\n4.  Discuss anesthetic technique for resection of pheo.\n5.  Describe management of hypertensive crisis associated with pheochromocytoma.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1.  Pheochromocytomas (pheos) are catecholamine secreting tumors that arise from chromaffin cells of the sympatho-adrenal system. The etiology of pheos is unknown. 90-95% are isolated while 5-10% are inherited. 90% are located in the adrenal medulla. Most pheos secrete norepinephrine with epinephrine in a ratio of 85/15. \n2.  Hypertension is the most common manifestation of the disease. Headache, sweating, pallor, and palpitations are classic signs and symptoms. Patients have increased systemic vascular resistance, usually normal cardiac output, and decreased plasma volume. Catechol-induced cardiomyopathy may also occur. The cardiomyopathy appears to be reversible if catechol stimulation is removed early before fibrosis has occurred.\n3.  Preoperative management emphasizes alpha blockade with phenoxybenzamine since most pheos secrete norepinephrine. Beta blockade can be added if the patient develops tachycardia with alpha blockade. Alpha-methyl-tyrosine inhibits catecholamine synthesis. Calcium channel blockers and ACE inhibitors may also be used to control hypertension. \n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1.  Alpha-block, rehydrate, +/- beta-block\n2.  Premed with midazolam\n3.  Radial artery and pulmonary artery catheters\n4.  Preoxygenate\n5.  Tracheal intubation following narcotics, induction agent and vecuronium\n6.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, isoflurane, narcotics, and relaxant.\n7.  Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n8.  Monitor arterial blood gas, glucose, urine output, ECG and temperature\n9.  Nitroprusside available for hypertensive crisis\n10. Adequate fluid replacement is essential. Hypotension following tumor vein ligation is treated with fluids. To prevent precipitous hypotension, volume expansion to pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 16-18 mmHg should be attained prior to vein ligation.\n11. Dextrose containing solution is added after tumor removal\n12. Certain drugs should be theoretically avoided:\na.  Halothane sensitizes the myocardium to catecholamines\nb.  Morphine, curare, atracurium can cause histamine release\nc.  Atropine, pancuronium, succinylcholine are vagolytic or sympathomimetic and may stimulate sympathetic nervous system\nd.  Droperidol, chlorpromazine, metoclopramide, and ephedrine can cause significant hypertensive responses\n13.  Fear, pain, shivering, hypoxia, and hypercarbia must be minimized to avoid provocation of the tumor\n14.  Dysrhythmias are usually ventricular in origin and best managed with lidocaine and esmolol.\n\nTreatment of Hypertensive Crisis in Pheo:\n1.  Alpha blockade with phentolamine (2- 5 mg IV increments) or labetalol (5 mg increments) or direct acting vasodilation with nitroprusside (0.1 - 2 mcg/kg/min)\n2.  Rehydration with lactated ringer's solution\n3.  Beta-blockade with metoprolol (2.5 - 5 mg IV increments) or esmolol (500 mcg/kg load plus 50 - 200 mcg/kg/min)\n\nClinical Points:\n1.  Elective surgery is recommended to remove pheo if possible.\n2.  Optimal pre-op preparation with alpha adrenergic blockade +/- beta blockade, +/- alpha methylpara-tyrosine is neccessary. Never precede alpha blockade with beta blockade since heart failure may occur.\n3.  Avoid drugs or maneuvers which provoke catechol release.\n4.  Maintain cardiovascular stability with short acting drugs.\n5.  If a catecholamine-induced cardiomyopathy is present monitor the patient with a pulmonary artery catheter.\n6.  The choice of anesthetic is not as crucial as the understanding with which the drugs are used. Certain drugs are to be avoided on a theoretical basis.\n7.  Persistent hypertension, hypotension, and hypoglycemia are common in the postoperative period and must be treated aggressively.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 25, 2013\n\n", 
