+    "leanBodyMass": 52, 
+    "learningObj": "34 year old female for partial thyroidectomy (52 kg)\n\nThis patient has fatigue and weight loss, plus lab results consistent with mild hyperthyroidism. Elective surgery should not be done until the patient is euthyroid. Beta-adrenergic blockade should be used to control the hyperdynamic cardiovascular system.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  List the differential diagnosis of tachycardia under general anesthesia.\n2.  List the most common causes of hyperpyrexia under general anesthesia.\n3.  Distinguish presenting signs of thyroid storm and malignant hyperthermia.\n4.  Describe treatment for thyroid storm.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1.  Inadequately treated hyperthyroidism - obtain medical consultation and consider delaying the surgery.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1.  Delay surgery until hyperthyroidism is controlled with antithyroid drugs such as propylthiouracil and beta-blockade\n2.  Routine monitoring. Must monitor temperature and place patient on cooling mattress.\n3.  Preoxygenate\n4.  Tracheal intubation following induction with narcotics, hypnotic and succinylcholine\n5.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, and isoflurane.\n6.  Avoid ketamine, pancuronium, and halothane due their effects on the sympathetic nervous system.\n7.  Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nThyroid Storm:\nThyroid storm, or thyrotoxicosis, is a sudden massive release of thyroid hormones leading to hyperthermia, tachycardia, dehydration, congestive heart failure, and shock. It usually occurs 6-18 hours post-op, but may occur intraoperatively. The hyper-metabolic state, tachycardia, and hyperthermia can be difficult to distinguish from malignant hyperthermia. Patients with thyroid storm are often hyperglycemic due to thyroid hormone induced increase in glycogenolysis and decreased insulin release.\n\nTreatment for Thyroid Storm:\n1.  Cold crystalloid with glucose\n2.  Beta-blockade\n3.  Sodium iodide 500-1000 mg IV every 8 hrs\n4.  Cortisol 100-200 mg IV every 8 hrs\n5.  Propylthiouracil 200-400 mg p.o. every 8 hrs\n6.  Avoid the use of aspirin to lower temperature since it can displace thyroxine from proteins.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 26, 2013\n\n", 
