+    "learningObj": "\n27 year old female with fractured humerus (52 kg)\n\nThis patient has no significant past medical history, allergies, or family history of  anesthetic complications. Physical exam and lab studies are unremarkable.  Her recent meal is of concern.\n\nIn this simulation you need to perform rapid sequence induction and provide adequate general anesthesia through surgical incision.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. Describe the techniques and drugs to reduce the incidence and severity of aspiration of gastric contents.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1. Full stomach - prevention of aspiration.\n\nSuggested Management\n1. Routine monitoring with electrocardiogram, non-invasive blood pressure, pulse oximeter and capnogram.\n2. Preoxygenate the patient until the end-tidal oxygen level is 60-80%.\n3. Apply cricoid pressure. Although the value of cricoid pressure is controversial, we have elected to retain this maneuver for this case scenario.\n4. Administer propofol 2 mg/kg (150 mg) IV bolus and succinylcholine 1.5 mg/kg (120 mg) IV bolus.\n5. After the patient becomes apneic, intubate the trachea and apply positive pressure ventilation.\n6. Listen to the breath sounds and observe the capnogram to confirm proper placement of the endotracheal tube.\n7. Remove cricoid pressure.\n8. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics and inhalation agent.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: July 25, 2013\n\n", 
