+CaseData[20] = {
+    "title": "Case 20 - 51 year old for emergency exploratory laparotomy", 
+    "badDrugs": ["Dantrolene"], 
+    "coMin": 1.2, 
+    "debriefMsg": "\nThe differential diagnosis for intubated but hard to ventilate includes obstruction of the endotracheal tube due to kinking, plugging, or positioning; and problems with the breathing circuit such as obstruction or malfunctioning valve. You must rule out these problems before assuming that the problem is bronchospasm.\n\nIn this case the problem is endobronchial intubation. Notice that the airway pressure is high with positive pressure ventilation since only one lung is ventilated. The arterial oxygen saturation declines due to shunted blood through the unventilated lung.To make the diagnosis, listen to the breath sounds, check the insertion depth of the tube, and/or perform fiberoptic bronchoscopy through the tube to locate the carina in relation to the tip of the tube.",
+    "depthCarina": 24, 
+    "depthCords": 18, 
+    "depthTubeInitial": 25, 
+    "durationPrep": 60, 
+    "durationSurgery": 1200, 
+    "helpFlag": true, 
+    "imageSet": 4, 
+    "initialSat": 96, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 70, 
+    "learningObj": "\n51 year old for emergency exploratory laparotomy (85 kg)\n\nThis patient was involved in a motor vehicle accident. The patient has intraperitoneal bleeding with possible splenic injury. Suspect concurrent, undiagnosed injuries in trauma patients. In this simulated case, remember even in complicated cases, simple things can go wrong.\n\nIn this simulation you need to perform rapid sequence induction and provide adequate general anesthesia for surgery. An emergency will occur during the operation which you must diagnose and treat.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. State the differential diagnosis of 'intubated but hard to ventilate'.\n2. Understand the pathophysiology of pneumothorax\n3. List ways to differentiate pneumothorax from endobronchial intubation.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations:\nTrauma - rule out other injuries\nAcutely intoxicated and combative\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring, plus direct arterial line for blood pressure and labs, and foley catheter for urine output\n2. Consider central line for venous access and filling pressure\n3. Preoxygenate\n4. Rapid sequence induction with narcotics, hypnotic agent, and succinylcholine. Titrate carefully to avoid hypotension if patient is hypovolemic.\n5. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, inhalation agent and relaxant.\n6. Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Intubated but Hard to Ventilate\n1. Kinked or obstructed endotracheal tube - mucus plug, clot, foreign object.\n2. Light anesthesia with bucking\n3. Endobronchial intubation\n4. Esophageal intubation\n5. Bronchospasm\n6. Tension pneumothorax\n\nPneumothorax:\n1. The presence of air within the pleural space can compress lung volume and can cause mediastinal shift.  V/Q mismatching can lead to hypoxemia, while increased intrathoracic pressure and mediastinal rotation can impede venous return leading to hypotension.\n2. The most common causes of pneumothorax include:\na. Chest trauma\nb. Pleural puncture during subclavian and internal jugular venopuncture, or other procedure\nc. Surgical incision of visceral pleura.\nd. Spontaneous rupture of bleb\ne. High inspiratory pressure\n\nMaking the Diagnosis of Pneumothorax:\n1. Hyper-resonance, positive scratch sign and more harsh breath sounds over affected side\n2. Tracheal deviation\n3. Shifted cardiac impulse\n4. Subcutaneous emphysema\n5. X-ray if chance\n6. Insert needle anterior second intercostal space\n\nMaking the Diagnosis of Endobronchial Intubation\n1. Typically the tube slips into the right mainstem and the breath sounds are louder on the right than the left.\n2. Endotracheal tube insertion should usually be 23 cm in adult males and 22 cm in adult females. Check tube insertion depth.\n3. When in doubt perform fiberoptic intubation and identify the carina below the tip of the tube.\n4. X-ray should rarely be necessary to confirm endotracheal tube position.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington\nDate of Last Modification: July 7, 2013\n\n", 
