+    "title": "Case 21 - 53 year old for emergency exploratory laparotomy", 
+    "badDrugs": ["Dantrolene"], 
+    "coMin": 1.2, 
+    "debriefMsg": "\nThe differential diagnosis for intubated but hard to ventilate includes obstruction of the endotracheal tube due to kinking, plugging, or positioning; and problems with the breathing circuit such as obstruction or malfunctioning valve. You must rule out these problems before assuming that the problem is bronchospasm.\n\nIn this case the problem is pneumothorax. Pneumothorax often worsens with positive pressure ventilation and must be ruled out in cases of high intrathoracic pressure and hypotension. Supine chest film may not demonstrate the pneumothorax.\n\n", 
+    "depthCarina": 25, 
+    "depthCords": 18, 
+    "depthTubeInitial": 22, 
+    "durationPrep": 60, 
+    "durationSurgery": 2400, 
+    "helpFlag": true, 
+    "imageSet": 4, 
+    "initialSat": 96, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 70, 
+    "learningObj": "\n53 year old for emergency exploratory laparotomy (85 kg)\n\n This patient was involved in a motor vehicle accident. Suspect concurrent injuries in trauma patients.\n\nIn this simulation you need to perform rapid sequence induction and provide adequate general anesthesia for surgery. An emergency will occur during the operation which you must diagnose and treat.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. State the differential diagnosis of hypoxemia in trauma\n2. Understand the pathophysiology of pneumothorax\n3. Understand the functioning of chest drainage\n\nAnesthetic Considerations:\nTrauma - rule out other injuries\nAcutely intoxicated and combative\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring, plus direct arterial line for blood pressure and labs, and foley for urine output\n2. Consider central line for venous access and filling pressure\n3. Preoxygenate\n4. Rapid sequence induction with narcotics, hypnotic agent, and succinylcholine. Titrate carefully to avoid hypotension if patient is hypovolemic.\n5. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, inhalation agent and relaxant.\n6. Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for High Airway Pressure\n1. Kinked or obstructed endotracheal tube - mucus plug, clot, foreign object.\n2. Light anesthesia with bucking\n3. Endobronchial intubation\n4. Esophageal intubation\n5. Bronchospasm\n6. Tension pneumothorax\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Hypotension:\n1. Hypovolemia\n2. Tension pneumothorax\n3. Pericardial tamponade\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Hypoxia:\n1.  Inadequate FiO2\n2.  Inadequate ventilation\na.  Airway obstruction\nb.  Esophageal intubation\nc.  Decreased respiratory drive due to drugs or head injury\n3.  V/Q mismatach - Shunt\na.  Lung contusion\nb.  Bronchial obstruction due to tooth or blood clot\nc.  Pneumothorax\nd.  Pulmonary edema\n\nPneumothorax:\n1. The presence of air within the pleural space can compress lung volume and can cause mediastinal shift.  V/Q mismatching can lead to hypoxemia, while increased intrathoracic pressure and mediastinal rotation can impede venous return leading to hypotension.\n2. The most common causes of pneumothorax include:\na. Chest trauma\nb. Pleural puncture during subclavian and internal jugular venopuncture, or other procedure\nc. Surgical incision of visceral pleura.\nd. Spontaneous rupture of bleb\ne. High inspiratory pressure\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Wheezing:\n1. Obstructed endotracheal tube\n2. Endobronchial intubation\n3. Bronchospasm\n4. Anaphylaxis\n5. Aspiration\n6. Pulmonary edema\n7. Pulmonary embolism\n8. Pneumothorax\n\nClinical Presentation\n1. Wheezing\n2. Increased airway pressure\n3. Decreased beath sounds\n4. Cyanosis may occur with severe V/Q mismatch\n5. Hypotension with reflex tachycardia\n\nMaking the Diagnosis:\n1. Hyper-resonance, positive scratch sign and more harsh breath sounds over affected side\n2. Tracheal deviation\n3. Shifted cardiac impulse\n4. Subcutaneous emphysema\n5. X-ray if chance\n6. Insert needle anterior second intercostal space\n\nTreatment:\nAspiration with chest tube\n\nClinical Points:\n1. Supine chest films may not demonstrate pneumothoraces as the air is distributed anteriorly over the lung surface and the lung/pleura separation will not be seen.\n2. Pneumothorax frequently becomes worse after institution of positive pressure ventilation.\n3. Use of nitrous oxide will increase the size of the pneumothorax as the gas diffuses in more rapidly than nitrogen diffuses out.\n4. Tension will increase intra-thoracic pressures and will reduce venous return. This is much more marked in a patient with already compromised intravascular volume.\n5. Any occlusion of the thoracostomy drainage will result in a recurrence of the pneumothorax if the original leak has not sealed.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 26, 2013\n\n", 
