+    "learningObj": "\n24 yr old male with penetrating eye injury and full stomach (76 kg)\n\nThis patient has a full stomach and a penetrating eye injury.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Describe anesthetic concerns for the patient with an open eye injury.\n2.  Describe the management of a patient with an open eye injury and a full stomach.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1.  Full stomach - prevention of aspiration\n2.  Describe the management of a patient with an open eye injury and a full stomach.\n3.  Penetrating eye injury - avoid ketamine, etomidate. Succinylcholine appears not to be a problem after defasciculating dose of competitive neuromuscular blocker.\n\nSuccinylcholine and Penetrating Eye Injury\n1.  Intraocular pressure increases from contraction of extraocular muscles with succinylcholine. Vitreous expulsion with loss of vision is rare but has been reported.\n2.  Libotani reported 2000 cases in which succinylcholine was administered with curare pretreatment in penetrating eye injury without vitreous expulsion.\n3.  Although vecuronium does not raise intraocular pressure, it does rise during intubation. (Anesth Analg 1986; 65: 877)\n4.  Although controversial, there is no evidence that a competitive neuromuscular blocker offers advantages over succinylcholine to facilitate tracheal intubation in patients with an open eye injury and a full stomach.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1.  Routine monitoring\n2.  Preoxygenate\n3.  Pretreatment with defasciculating dose of competitive neuromuscular blocker \n4.  Tracheal intubation following rapid sequence induction with propofol and succinylcholine or rocuronium while applying cricoid pressure.\n\nNote:\nAwake intubation requires extremely thorough topical anesthesia to avoid coughing.\n\nReferences\n1.  Libotani: Succinylcholine in open eye surgery. Anesthesiology 1985; 62: 637\n2.  Berry, Merin: Etomidate myoclonus and the open globe. Anesth Analg 1989; 69: 256  \n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 27, 2013\n\n", 
