+CaseData[24] = {
+    "title": "Case 24 - 22 year old with septic knee and diabetic ketoacidosis", 
+    "badDrugs": ["Dantrolene"], 
+    "coMin": 1.2, 
+    "debriefMsg": "\nThis patient is severely dehydrated. You should administer 1-2 liters of intravenous fluids prior to induction since hypovolemic patients can become extremely hypotensive on induction. Administer insulin also and monitor blood glucose, potassium and pH closely.\n\n", 
+    "depthCarina": 25, 
+    "depthCords": 18, 
+    "depthTubeInitial": 22, 
+    "durationPrep": 60, 
+    "durationSurgery": 1200, 
+    "helpFlag": true, 
+    "imageSet": 2, 
+    "initialSat": 96, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 72, 
+    "learningObj": "\n22 year old with septic knee and diabetic ketoacidosis (72 kg)\n\nThis patient has diabetic ketoacidosis and requires emergency surgery. It is usually advisable to delay surgery until after the fluid and electrolyte imbalances associated with diabetic ketoacidosis are corrected slowly.  Surgery is urgent in this patient with a septic joint so the fluid and electrolyte therapy would be corrected faster, over a couple hours.  In this simulation you need to replace intravascular volume, administer insulin, and monitor glucose, potassium and acidosis prior to induction. For this simulation you can replace the fluids in a few minutes even though in clinical practice the fluid and electrolyte imbalance would be corrected over 2 hours. \n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Describe the anesthetic considerations for a patient with diabetes mellitus.\n2.  Describe perioperative management of the diabetic patient.\n3.  Describe the metabolic derangements and management of diabetic ketoacidosis.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations for Diabetes Mellitus\nDetermine extent of disease:\n1.  Ketoacidosis\n2.  Neuropathy\na.  Autonomic dysfunction -\n    1) orthostatic hypotension\n    2) small heart rate response to hypotension, atropine\nb. Peripheral neuropathy\n3.  Atherosclerosis - coronary, cerebral, peripheral\n4.  Nephropathy\n5.  Retinopathy\n6.  Prone to infection\n\nManagement of Diabetic Patient\n1.  Identify end-organ damage\n2.  Avoid surgery if ketoacidosis is present\n3.  Avoid hypoglycemia - by administration of glucose\n4.  Avoid ketoacidosis - by administration of insulin\n\nManagement of Diabetic Ketoacidosis\n1.  Delay surgery if possible\n2.  Isotonic saline 2 - 3 liters over 2 hours\n3.  Insulin 0.1 U/kg per hr IV initially, followed by continuous insulin infusion at 1 - 10 Units per hour\n4.  Sodium bicarbonate if pH < 7.10 and acidosis is life-threatening.\n5.  KCl 40 meq/hr added to saline if K+ < 3.0, monitor K+ closely.  You must administer potassium exceedingly cautiously. \n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan for This Simulated Case\n1.  Delay surgery if possible\n2.  Routine monitoring plus radial artery catheter\n3.  Replace fluids. If you decide to administer potassium you must do so exceedingly cautiously. Notice that potassium is not even available in this simulator program.\n4.  Insulin 20 Units Regular IV\n5.  Preoxygenate\n6.  Tracheal intubation following rapid sequence induction with narcotics, etomidate, and vecuronium while applying cricoid pressure\n7.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, and sevoflurane\n8.  Extubate after return of airway reflexes\n\nReferences\n1. Foster: Metabolic derangements and treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis. N Engl J Med 1983; 309: 159\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of CA-Irvine \nDate of Last Modification: June 17, 2024\n\n", 
+    "possiblePoints": 1000, 
+    "preopMsg": "\n22 year old with septic knee and diabetic ketoacidosis\n\nHistory of Present Illness\nDiabetic patient has fevers and chills with a painful, swollen, and inflamed knee.\n\nPast Medical History\nIllnesses: Insulin dependent diabetes mellitus\nSurgeries: none\nMeds: Insulin 20 Units NPH and 10 Units regular every morning plus 10 Units NPH and 5 Units regular every evening\nAllergies: none\nHabits: smokes, drinks, and uses intravenous drugs\n\nFamily History\nNo family history of anesthetic problems.\n\nPhysical Exam\nThin male, somewhat confused, anxious, and hyperventilating\nWeight: 72 Kg   T: 39.2   HR: 122   BP: 106/60   RR: 24\nAirway: normal\nLungs : clear  \nHeart : normal\n\nLabs\nHCT: 48    WBC : 18.2  Glucose: 548\nNa: 135     K: 3.8    Cl: 102    HCO3: 14    BUN: 10  Cr: 0.8\n\nNPO Status\nThe patient last ate 13 hours ago. \n\nPatient and Surgeon Requests\nThe patient refuses regional anesthesia. The surgeon states the procedure will take 1 hour.\n\n", 
+    "volAnDsp": 80, 
+    "states": [
+        {
+            "name": "DRY", 
