+    "learningObj": "\n4 year old for tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy (35 kg)\n\nThis patient has a significant history of sleep apnea. \n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Understand the syndrome of sleep apnea.\n2.  Understand how sleep apnea affects induction of anesthesia.\n3.  Understand the postoperative management of sleep apnea.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1.  Routine monitoring\n2.  Establish intravenous access\n3.  Preoxygenate\n4.  Consider awake fiberoptic intubation since airway appears to be difficult. You can also consider intubation following inhalation induction while maintaining spontaneous ventilation.\n5.  Maintain anesthesia with oxygen and inhalation agent\n6.  Awake extubation is indicated.\n\nClinical Points\n1.  Children with obstructive sleep apnea are scheduled for surgery because they have significant problems with airway obstruction. \n2.  Obesity, pauses in breathing, and daytime somnolence suggest moderate to severe sleep apnea and obstruction.\n3.  Avoid premedication as may be more sensitive to respiratory depression.\n4.  Establish intravenous access prior to inhalation induction.\n5.  Oral airway may relieve airway obstruction.\n6.  Opioids should be avoided and titrated carefully if needed in the postoperative period.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: November 22, 2013\n\n", 
