+    "learningObj": "\n86 year old female with fractured hip (46 kg)\n\nThis patient has a number of potentially important problems.   Advanced age is often associated with reduced myocardial performance, and decreased ventilatory function causing an increased susceptibility to the depressant effects of anesthetic agents. Furthermore, a decline in renal and hepatic function lead to prolonged elimination of drugs and increase the likelihood of adverse drug interactions.\n\nThis patient's history, physical exam, and laboratory studies all indicate severe hypovolemia. Preoperative fluid replacement and monitoring of cardiac filling pressures and urine output is suggested. Inadequate preoperative fluid replacement may lead to hypotension on induction.\n\n In this simulation you need to replace intravascular volume, induce anesthesia and provide adequate general anesthesia in preparation for the surgical incision.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. How is hypovolemia recognized prior to induction and why is this important?\n2. List the differential diagnosis of hypotension under general anesthesia and describe your management approach.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1. Advanced age\n2. Hypovolemia\n\nSuggested Management\n1. Routine monitoring, plus intraarterial and central venous pressure measurements and urine output.\n2. Volume replacement. Optimally, intravascular volume deficits would be corrected slowly, but for this simulation please proceed with the case after rapid rehydration.\n3. Preoxygenate\n4. Judicious titration of narcotic, hypnotic and relaxant followed by tracheal intubation.\n5. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, and inhaled agent as tolerated.\n6. Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nRecognition of Hypovolemia\n1. Estimation of fluid in's and out's.\n2. Oliguria\n3. Orthostatic hypotension\n4. SPV - Systolic Pressure Variation \n5. Low filling pressures - CVP, PAWP\n6. Tachycardia\n7. Hypotension\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Hypotension\n1. Preload\na. Hypovolemia\nb. Venous dilation secondary to medications\nc. Obstructed venous return - compression\nd. Increased intrathoracic pressure\n\n2. Pump - rate, rhythm, contractility\na. Myocardial ischemia\nb. Anesthetic agents\n\n3. Afterload\na. Sympathetic block\nb. Medications\n\n4. Mixed\na. Anaphylactic reaction\nb. Transfusion reaction\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 6, 2013\n\n", 
