+CaseData[30] = {
+    "title": "Case 30 - 4 year old for myringotomy and tubes", 
+    "badDrugs": ["Nitroglycerin"], 
+    "coMin": 1.2, 
+    "debriefMsg": "\nIn this simulation you should learn to recognize the clinical presentation of malignant hyperthermia and distinguish from other causes of hyperpyrexia.\n\nYou must treat aggressively by discontinuing the inhalation anesthetic agent, securing the airway, hyperventilating with 100% oxygen, rapid administration of cold saline and administration of dantrolene. You can also successfully complete this case by avoiding potent inhalation agent and succinylcholine, but you will learn more about the management of MH crisis if you trigger the reaction.\n\n", 
+    "depthCarina": 18, 
+    "depthCords": 12, 
+    "depthTubeInitial": 15, 
+    "durationPrep": 60, 
+    "durationSurgery": 2400, 
+    "helpFlag": true, 
+    "imageSet": 1, 
+    "initialSat": 96, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 18, 
+    "learningObj": "\n4 year old for myringotomy and tubes (18 kg)\nThis patient has ear infections and other than strabismus and vague muscle pains, has an unremarkable past medical history and family history.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  List preop questions and screening to diagnose possible malignant hyperthermia susceptibility.\n2.  List presenting signs of malignant hyperthermia.\n3.  Describe identification and treatment of hyperkalemia.\n4.  Describe diagnosis and treatment of malignant hyperthermia.\n5.  Describe treatment of post-malignant hyperthermia complications.\n\nMalignant Hyperthermia\n1.  MH is an inherited disease characterized by a hypermetabolic state of skeletal muscle caused by exposure to an anesthetic triggering agent. \n2.  Increased intracellular calcium ion concentrations cause hypermetabolism. \n3.  An abnormality in the ryanidine receptor is the cause of MH in susceptible pigs and appears to be the likely cause of MH in humans. About 5% of humans with MH susceptibility have specific genetic defects associated with the ryanidine receptor (RYR1). Other specific genetic defects on the ryanidine receptor, Na channels, DHP receptor are presently under investigation.\n\nPreoperative Evaluation\n1.  Muscle aches and pains, strabismus, and scoliosis are no more common in the malignant hyperthermia population than the normal population. \n2.  Preop history of suspicious reaction in the patient or a family member may be the only indicators of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility. Even after extensive  questioning many MH patients have a negative personal and family history.\n3.  Muscular dystrophy may correlate with malignant hyperthermia susceptibility.\n4.  Muscle diseases such as central core disease, carnitine palmityl transferase deficiency, and King-Denborough syndrome may strongly correlate with MH.\n\nPresenting Signs of Acute MH Crisis\nFollowing administration of succinylcholine or potent inhalation agent :\n1. Muscle rigidity may occur rather than relaxation\n2. Tachycardia is a consistent sign unless patient is on a beta-blocker\n3. Hypertension may occur early\n4. Cardiac arrhythmias may occur\n5. Marked increase in CO2 production -> respiratory acidosis\n6. Hypermetabolic tissues exceed O2 supply -> metabolic acidosis\n7. Erythematous rash progressing to mottled cyanosis\n\nLater Signs of Acute MH Crisis\n1. Pyrexia - temp may rise as much as 1 degree C every 5 min.\n2. Hypoxemia may occur with increased O2 consumption\n3. Potassium levels may initially rise\n4. Calcium levels may fluctuate\n5. Creatinine kinase levels increase markedly\n6. Myoglobinuria is common\n7. Renal failure\n8. Left ventricular failure and pulmonary edema\n9. Disseminated intravascular coagulation\n10.Cerebral edema\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Hyperpyrexia\n1. Malignant hyperthermia\n2. Sepsis - consider intravenous contaminant\n3. Overheating - warming blanket, lights, etc.\n4. Hypothalamic injury\n5. Thyrotoxicosis\n6. Faulty temperature measurement\n7. Osteogenesis imperfecta\n8. Transfusion reaction\n9. Drug reactions: neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), MAO inhibitors, amphetamine, cocaine, tricyclics, atropine, glycopyrrolate, droperidol, metoclopramide, levodopa withdrawal, cogentin, ketamine, EtOH withdrawal, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)\n\nMasseter Spasm and MH\n1. Masseter spasm occurs in as many as 1% of children receiving halothane and succinylcholine.\n2. Masseter spasm is often, but not always seen following succinylcholine administration in patients that develop MH.\n3. Masseter spasm could be considered an early sign of possible MH crisis, especially if other muscle rigidity is present.\n4. Muscle rigidity may develop more slowly during anesthetic if succinylcholine is not used.\n\nTreatment of MH Crisis\n1. Stop inhalation anesthesia\n2. Hyperventilate with 100 % oxygen\n3. Administer dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg as quickly as possible. Continue to give dantrolene until all signs of MH have resolved. This will decrease the incidence of recrudescence. A total dose of 20 mg/kg may be necessary.\n4. Administer cold intravenous fluids - use normal saline, not ringer's lactate\n5. Follow arterial blood gas, electrolytes and glucose. Central venous gases are also sensitive in identifying tissue hypoxia and hypercarbia.\n6. Do not administer calcium channel blockers. They can cause cardiac arrest and hyperkalemia in the presence of dantrolene.\n7. Monitor for relapse\n8. Call the MH Hotline for assistance: 1-800-MH-HYPER\n\nManagement of the MH Susceptible Patient\n1. Avoid triggering agents: succinylcholine and potent inhalation agents\n2. Pretreatment with dantrolene is controversial and usually not indicated\n3. Use cleansed anesthesia machine and fresh circuit. If machine is purged for 20 minutes then it is not necessary to change the CO2 absorbent.\n4. Have MH cart ready with 36 vials of dantrolene imediately available.\n5. MH patients can be managed as outpatients as long as appropriate precautions are taken and the patient is monitored in the recovery room for four hours.\n\nClinical Points\n1.  Large fresh gas flows in a Bain circuit may cause large gradients between arterial and ET-CO2. Thus, a malignant hyperthermia episode can occur when ET-CO2 is \"normal\".  Arterial blood gas would demonstrate respiratory acidosis.\n2.  The signs and symptoms of MH point to a hypermetabolic state that is similar but not identical to thyroid storm. In thyroid storm the degree of acidosis is less, peak temperatures are lower, and peak CPK levels are lower. Thyroid storm does not respond to dantrolene.\n3.  Neuroleptic malignant syndrome may mimic an MH-like reaction but evolves over an extended period of time and is associated with a history of phenothiazine or butyrophenone use.\n4.  In the past couple years there have been approximately 30 children who have suffered a hyperkalemic cardiac arrest. This seems to be due to an underlying myopathy. This can be part of the malignant hyperthermia syndrome but is not necessarily associated with MH. Hyperkalemia should be thought of with any apparently healthy child who has a cardiac arrest after the use of succinylcholine.  Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest has rarely occurred in children without succinylcholine. Appropriate treatment includes rapid identification, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, hyperventilation, calcium chloride, glucose, insulin, and bicarbonate. CPR should be continued for prolonged periods of time since a complete recovery is possible.\n\nReferences\n1.  Larach: Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest during anesthesia in children with occult myopathies. Clin Pediatr 1997; 36: 9-16\n2.  O'Flynn: Masseter muscle rigidity and MH susceptibility. Anesthesiology 1994; 80: 1228-1233\n3.  Heiman-Patterson: Neuroleptic malignant syndrome and malignant hyperthermia. Med Clin North Am 1993; 77: 477-492\n4.  Nimmo: Drug induced hyperthermia. Anesthesia 1993; 48: 892-895\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 28, 2013\n\n", 
