+    "learningObj": "\n23 year old preeclamptic for induction of labor followed by Cesarean delivery (78 kg)\n\nG1P0 at 30 weeks estimated gestational age. She is receiving an oxytocin infusion to induce labor because of worsening preeclampsia and concerns of fetal well being.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  List the diagnostic criteria for severe preeclampsia.\n2.  List pathophysiogical alterations in preeclampsia.\n3.  List potential problems associated with analgesia for labor and anesthesia for cesarean delivery in patients with preeclampsia.\n\nPathophysiology of Preeclampsia\n1.  The cause of preeclampsia is unknown and there is no good animal model of the human form of the disease. Proposed etiologies include infection, autoimmune, ischemia, hemodynamic, humoral toxins. A current popular theory proposes that an imbalance in the prostaglandins prostacyclin (promotes vasodilation) and thromboxane (promotes vasoconstriction) result in the disease manifestations.  This imbalance may stem from endothelial damage.\n2.  The disease is a multisystem disorder characterized by decreased organ perfusion:\na) Decreased placental perfusion compromises fetal well-being\nb) Vasoconstriction results in hypertension, decreased intravascular volume and hemoconcentration.  Cardiac output is usually high initially, then may decline in severe and forms as systemic vascular resistance increases.  The vasculature may be hyperresponsive to vasopressors.\nc) Platelet activation and consumption results in thrombocytopenia.\nd) Pulmonary edema may result from capillary leak, low plasma oncotic pressure, or cardiac failure from severe hypertension and increased systemic vascular resistence.  Patients with underlying chronic hypertension or other systemic disease are at highest risk for pulmonary edema.\ne) Renal effects include decreased GFR, proteinuria, and if severe, elevated creatinine.\nf) Decreased plasma proteins contributes to diffuse interstitial edema\ng) Vasospasm may cause CNS symptoms (headache) and lead to seizures (eclampsia)\nh) Hepatocellur injury may present as HELLP (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) syndrome\n\nDiagnostic Criteria for Preeclampsia\n1.  The following triad occuring after the 20th week of gestation:\na) Hypertension - systolic > 140 or 30 mmHg above baseline diastolic > 90 or 15 mmHg above baseline\nb) Proteinuria\nc) Generalized edema\n2.  Associated findings of any of the following make the diagnosis of severe preeclampsia: Systolic > 160 or diastolic > 110 mmHg,  visual or neurologic signs or symptoms, epigastric pain (reflecting liver involvement), pulmonary edema, oliguria,  urine protein > 5 gms per 24 hours, HELLP syndrome.\n3.  Ecclampsia is present when the disease is complicated by seizures.\n4.  Risk factors: extremes of age, first pregnancies, chronic hypertension or vascular disease, low socioeconomic status and poor prenatal care, large placental mass (multiple gestation, macrosomia), prior pregnancy with preeclampsia.\n\nTreatment of Preeclampsia\n1.  Treatment of preeclampsia is delivery. If the fetus is unlikely to have reached lung maturity then treatment is supportive (control of hypertension, improve organ perfusion) until fetal lung maturity is reached or until the disease becomes a significant threat to the mother.\n2.  Supportive therapy includes bed rest, blood pressure control, maintenance fluids, and intravenous magnesium for seizure prophylaxis.\n3.  Blood pressure control is usually with hydralazine, sometimes with beta blockers.\n4.  Signs of the disease usually begin to resolve rapidly following delivery (2-3 days).\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1.  Anesthetic challenges include maintaining maternal hemodynamic stability (avoiding significant decreases in maternal blood pressure with regional analgesia/anesthesia and avoiding dangerous hypertension with induction of general anesthesia).  Maintaining stable maternal hemodynamics and proper maternal positioning optimizes the fetal condition.\n2.  Invasive monitoring (arterial line, CVP or PA catheter (in cases of persistent oliguria or pulmonary edema)) can be helpful since the preeclamptic hemodynamic profile can range from high cardiac output and low SVR to low cardiac output and high SVR.\n3. Regional analgesia for labor or regional anesthesia for cesarean delivery can be safely accomplished in patients without overt coagulopathy.  Important principles include:  moderate prehydration (excessive crystalloid can contribute to edema), slow induction of sympathetic block, avoiding hypotension, maintaining proper maternal positioning, and monitoring the fetus for evidence of compromise.\n4.  Securing the airway during general anesthesia can be difficult due to upper airway edema.  This may not be predicted on airway exam.  Proper pre-treatment and induction are essential to avoid excessive hypertension and acute pulmonary edema. Pre-treatment with labetalol, nitroglycerin, or nitroprusside are all acceptable to bring the blood pressure into an acceptable range (diastolic less than 100 or 25% reduction in mean blood pressure).\n5.  Rapid sequence induction with cricoid pressure using thiopental or propofol is recommended.  Be aware of the significant potentiation of nondepolarizing muscle relaxants by intravenous magnesium. Monitor neuromuscular blockade during general anesthesia and administer very small doses if necessary.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 28, 2013\n\n", 
