+    "leanBodyMass": 80, 
+    "learningObj": "\n19 year old eclamptic for urgent cesarean section (84 kg)\n\nThis patient has ecclampsia and HELLP syndrome. Emergency cesarean delivery is planned for both maternal and fetal indications.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Define preeclampsia/ecclampsia \n2.  Describe the pathophysiology of the disease. \n3.  List at risk time periods for preeclamptic/ecclamptic patient undergoing general anesthesia.\n4.  List drugs useful to control hypertension.\n5.  Describe clinical significance of uncontrolled blood pressure.\n6.  Describe clinical significance of oliguria in the preeclamptic patient.\n7.  List factors that may contribute to pulmonary edema in the preeclamptic patient.\n8.  Describe the anesthetic implications of HELLP syndrome.\n\nPreeclampsia/Eclampsia\n1.  Difficult airway management due to airway edema\n2.  Severe hypertension during laryngoscopy and intubation as well as extubation -- severe hypertension risks decreased placental perfusion, pulmonary edema, cerebral hemorrhage.\n3.  Decrease in oxygenation during the apnea accompanying intubation.\n4.  Abnormal muscle relaxant response due to hypermagnesemia\n\nTreatment of Hypertension\n1.  General principles: bedrest, magnesium is an anticonvulsant not an antihypertensive\n2.  During labor: labetolol, hydralazine, alphamethyldopa, beta-blockers?, nifedipine?\n3.  To blunt blood pressure increases during intubation/emergence: adequate anesthesia, labetalol, trimethaphan, esmolol, lidocaine, fentanyl, nitroprusside\n4. Severe, untreated hypertension may lead to further declines in uteroplacental perfusion and fetal well-being, decreased cardiac output and pulmonary edema, cerebral hemorrhage.\n\nAssessment and Treatment of Oliguria in Preeclampsia\n1.  Accurate measurement - no urinary obstruction\n2.  Acute intravenous hydration using non-dextrose crystalloid (250-500 ml)\n3.  Central venous monitoring\n4.  Continued hydration if urine output remains low and CVP<4\n5.  Consider use of colloids\n6.  Pulmonary artery monitoring:\na.  If PAOP is low: fluids\nb.  If SVR is high: vasodilator\nc.  If cardiac output is low: dopamine\n\nFactors that Contribute to Pulmonary Edema\n1.  Decreased plasma proteins and colloid oncotic pressure\n2.  Capillary endothelial damage\n3.  Excessive infusion of fluids\n4.  CVP> 6 mmHg\n5.  Increased SVR (cardiac failure)\n6.  Relatively older in age\n7.  Patients with chronic hypertension or other systemic disease (such as diabetes) with superimposed preeclampsia.\n\nSignificance of HELLP syndrome\nHemolysis\nElevated Liver enzymes\nLow Platelets\n\n1.  Thrombocytopenia may be so severe as to necessitate general anesthesia. Resolution of thrombocytopenia may take up to 2 days.\n2.  Other coagulation factors may be abnormal.\n3.  May lead to need for cesarean delivery with risk of hemorrhage.\n4.  Hepatic swelling heralded by right upper quadrant pain, may lead to hepatic rupture.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: November 24, 2013\n\n", 
