+    "learningObj": "\n32 yr old at 24 weeks gestation for intracerebral aneurysm clipping (70 kg)\n\nThe patient is G2 P1 at 24 weeks gestation for non-obstetric surgery. She has an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  List the anesthetic considerations for anesthesia for operations during pregnancy.\n2.  List drugs to avoid and evidence that they may be teratogenic.\n3.  Describe means to avoid intrauterine fetal hypoxia.\n4.  Describe methods to prevent premature labor.\n\nGoals of Anesthesia for Operations during Pregnancy\n1.  Overall fetal demise is 5-35% when surgery is performed on pregnant women. Avoid elective surgery, postpone essential surgery until second or third trimester.\n2.  Avoid use of teratogenic drugs.\n3.  Avoid intrauterine hypoxia\n4.  Avoid premature labor\n\nPotentially Teratogenic Drugs\n1.  Most susceptible during organogenesis - 15 to 56 days of gestation\n2.  Regional technique may be preferred (if possible) - especially spinal since fetal drug exposure is minimal\n3.  Nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B-12 and methionine synthetase which can effect DNA synthesis. Studies show significant detrimental effects of nitrous oxide on fetal growth, skeletal development and death on pregnant rats and incubating chicks - best to avoid.\n4.  Halothane has been shown to be teratogenic in rat fetuses - may be best to avoid.\n5.  Use light premedication if any. Diazepam may be associated with oral cleft. Best to avoid.\n\nAvoid Intrauterine Hypoxia\n1.  Avoid maternal hypoxia and hypotension\n2.  Ephedrine is the vasopressor of choice - avoid phenylephrine since it raises maternal blood pressure but does not restore uterine blood flow. Recent studies show phenylephrine may be less of a problem than previously suspected.\n2.  Epinephrine and norepinephrine may also decrease uterine blood flow.\n3.  Ketamine in doses greater than 1 mg/kg can may dangerously increase uterine tone.\n4.  Protect against aspiration of gastric contents\n5.  Avoid supine position - use left uterine displacement\n6.  Avoid extreme hyperventilation which will decrease uterine blood flow.\n\nAvoid Premature Labor\n1.  Monitor fetal heart rate and uterine tone\n2.  Utilize tocolytic agents if necessary\na.  Beta-2 agonists - terbutaline, ritodrine\nb.  Magnesium sulfate\nc.  Ethanol\nd.  Prostaglandin inhibitors\ne.  Calcium channel blockers\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 29, 2013\n\n", 
