+    "leanBodyMass": 70, 
+    "learningObj": "\n22 year old healthy male (74 kg)\n\nThis patient has a history of hay fever and penicillin allergy so there is a slightly  increased risk for an allergic reaction. The patient may also be somewhat dehydrated and have an increased risk of aspiration with his acute abdomen.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. List the clinical manifestations of anaphylaxis.\n2. List the goals of treatment.\n3. List the steps you will take to treat anaphylaxis.\n4. State the appropriate dose of epinephrine for anaphylaxis and risk of administering too much.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\n1. Possible dehydration\n2. Slightly increased risk of allergic reaction\n3. Slightly increased risk of pulmonary aspiration\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring\n2. Preoxygenate\n3. Tracheal intubation following rapid sequence induction while applying cricoid pressure.\n4. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, inhalation agent.\n5. Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nManagement of Anaphylaxis\nAnaphylaxis can occur when prior exposure to a drug caused sensitization by producing IGE antibodies. Repeated exposure results in antigen - antibody reaction, leading to a release of histamine and other vasoactive substances from mast cells and basophils. It is not possible to predict which patients will experience an anaphylactic reaction, but patients with many prior exposures to the drug and those with allergic history have an increased risk. Muscle relaxants are the most commonly implicated anesthetic agents in anaphylaxis.\n\nAnaphylactoid reactions result from massive histamine release following administration of a drug. The reaction is not IGE mediated and does not require prior sensitization, but the clinical manifestations of an anaphylactoid reaction are otherwise indistinguishable from an anaphylactic reaction. These reactions are generally related to speed of injection and amount and type of drug administered.\n\nThe clinical manifestations of anaphylaxis usually occur within a few minutes of exposure to the triggering agent:\nHypotension: invariable\nTachycardia: invariable\nBronchospasm: common\nHypoxemia: common\nCardiac dysrhythmias: common\nLaryngeal and periorbital edema: common\nCutaneous flushing: common\nCoagulation defects: occasional\nLeukopenia: occasional\n\nNote: 75% of fatalities are due to bronchospasm\n\nGoals of Treatment\n1. Reverse arterial hypoxemia\n2. Replace intravascular volume\n3. Inhibit further release of vasoactive substances\n\nTreatment\n1. Supplemental oxygen\n2. Balanced salt solution or colloid - often rapid infusion of 1 - 4 liters required\n3. Epinephrine (initial dose 10 - 100 mcg IV)\n- Beta adrenergic effect inhibits degranulation and causes bronchodilation\n- Alpha effect causes vasoconstriction\n4. Diphenhydramine (initial dose 50 - 100 mg IV) to blunt histamine effects\n5. Albuterol inhaler (initial dose 10 puffs per ETT) for bronchospasm\n6. Phenylephrine (initial dose 25-100 mcg IV) - alpha adrenergic effect will raise BP\n7. Corticosteroids - Methylprednisolone (10-15 mg/kg) - may enhance beta effects of Epi, inhibit production of leukotrienes, and reduce reactions due to activation of complement system\n\nTreatment of Bronchospasm\n1. Hand ventilate to rule out mechanical problem with ventilator\n2. Check anesthesia circuit and valves - if possible use ambu bag\n3. Check location of endotracheal tube - auscultate and confirm end-tidal CO2\n4. Pass suction catheter or fiberoptic scope down tube to rule out obstruction\n5. Inhaled beta-agonists\n6. Deepen inhalation anesthesia as blood pressure allows\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 10, 2013\n\n", 
