+    "debriefMsg": "This patient has known coronary artery disease and is status-post myocardialinfarction 8 months ago. The patient currently has infrequent anginawith his current medical management consisting of atenolol, diltiazem,and nitroglycerin. For patients with stable angina: avoid hypoxia,avoid tachycardia, avoid hypotension, and monitor leads II and V5 closely.\n\nIf ST segment changes occur during the case, raise the blood pressure,lower the heart rate, and administer nitroglycerin. Treat the blood pressurefirst to provide adequate perfusion pressure. Raising the blood pressuremay also help to lower the heart rate and will make it easier to titratethe nitroglycerin without causing hypotension. Slow the heart rate withbeta-blocker, preferably esmolol, since the fast onset and recovery makeit easy to titrate. Opioids may also be helpful to slow the heart rateif pain is a factor. Nitroglycerin dilates coronary arteries to improve myocardial perfusion. It also dilates systemic veins, decreasing preload and myocardial oxygen demand.", 
+    "depthCarina": 25, 
