+    "learningObj": "\n67 year old male for femoral-popliteal bypass (82 kg)\n\nThis patient had a fairly recent myocardial infarction  and has continuing angina in a stable pattern. Dipyridamole-thallium scan shows two areas of myocardium still at risk. The patient refuses cardiac cath or surgical intervention so we are limited to medical management of his coronary artery disease. Good heart rate control and infrequent anginal episodes suggest that his medical management with a beta-blocker, calcium channel blocker and nitrates is adequate. \n\nThe patient refuses regional anesthesia.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. Describe the risk of reinfarction in the perioperative period.\n2. Describe the risks of using epidural or spinal anesthesia in patients that will receive heparin during their surgery.\n3. List treatment steps for acute myocardial ischemia.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations\nThe patient is at risk of a perioperative myocardial infarction. Reinfarctions are fatal in about half of cases and the reinfarction rate is dependent to a great extent on the time since the previous infarction:\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring, plus direct radial artery pressure monitoring\n2. Intravenous induction, secure the airway\n3. Titration of phenylephrine to maintain afterload if necessary \n\nManagement of ST Segment Changes\nAcute ST segment depression indicates probable myocardial ischemia. Ventricular dysrhythmias may also appear on the ECG. Sinus tachycardia, arterial hypotension, low cardiac output, and elevated filling pressures indicate left ventricular pump failure. Wall motion abnormalities on the transesophageal echo are another sensitive indicator of myocardial ischemia.\n\nTreatment of Myocardial Ischemia\n1. Oxygen\n2. If hypotension is present, administer phenylephrine (initial dose 100 mcg bolus or 1 mcg/kg/min infusion) to normalize blood pressure and coronary perfusion pressure.\n3. If blood pressure is normal or hypertension is present, administer nitroglycerin (initial dose 0.5-1.0 mcg/kg/min) to reduce myocardial oxygen demand and increase coronary blood flow.\n4. If tachycardia is present, slow heart rate to about 60 beats per minute with a beta-blocker. In this patient additional metoprolol (1 mg every 5 min) or an esmolol infusion would be appropriate.\n5. Add inotropic support if there is evidence of low cardiac output - decreased mental status, oliguria, persistent hypotension. Dobutamine (5-10 mcg/kg/min), epinephrine (0.01-0.05 mcg/kg/min), and dopamine 5-10 mcg/kg/min) are suitable first choices.\n6. Lidocaine if PVCs are present (1 mg/kg IV bolus then 1-4 mg/min infusion)\n7. Consider nifedipine to relieve coronary vasospasm manifested by ST segment elevation. Initial dose 10 mg sublingually.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, MD and G. Alec Rooke, MD, PhD\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 11, 2013\n\n", 
