+    "initialSat": 96, 
+    "leanBodyMass": 70, 
+    "learningObj": "\n32 year old for total abdominal hysterectomy (70 kg)\nThis patient has an unremarkable past medical history and has had uneventful general anesthesia in the past. The patient weighs 70 kg.\n\nThis is a simulation of a case that was reported in the Can Anaes Soc J 1966; 13: 437-442.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1. List the differential diagnosis of tachycardia under general anesthesia.\n2. List the most common causes of respiratory acidosis under general anesthesia.\n3. List the most common causes of hyperpyrexia under general anesthesia.\n4. Describe management of malignant hyperthermia.\n\nSuggested Anesthetic Plan\n1. Routine monitoring\n2. Rehydration\n3. Preoxygenate\n4. Tracheal intubation following induction with narcotics, propofol and succinylcholine.\n5. Maintain anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics, relaxant, and inhaled agent\n6. Extubate after return of airway reflexes.\n\nMalignant Hyperthermia\nMH is an inherited disease characterized by hypermetabolism caused by exposure to an anesthetic triggering agent. Increased intracellular calcium ion concentrations cause hypermetabolism, but the cause of the calcium release is unknown.\n\nClinical Points\n1. Family history may indicate anesthesia problems\n2. Crisis may not occur on first exposure to triggering agent\n3. Most prevalent in children\n\nPresenting Signs of Acute MH Crisis\nFollowing administration of succinylcholine or potent inhalation agent :\n1. Muscle rigidity may occur rather than relaxation\n2. Tachycardia is a consistent sign unless patient is on a beta-blocker\n3. Hypertension may occur early\n4. Cardiac arrhythmias may occur\n5. Marked increase in CO2 production -> respiratory acidosis\n6. Hypermetabolic tissues exceed O2 supply -> metabolic acidosis\n7. Erythematous rash progressing to mottled cyanosis\n\nLater Signs of Acute MH Crisis\n1. Pyrexia - temp may rise as much as 1 degree C every 5 min.\n2. Hypoxemia may occur with increased O2 consumption\n3. Potassium levels may initially rise\n4. Calcium levels may fluctuate\n5. Creatinine kinase levels increase markedly\n6. Myoglobinuria is common\n7. Renal failure\n8. Left ventricular failure and pulmonary edema\n9. Disseminated intravascular coagulation\n10.Cerebral edema\n\nDifferential Diagnosis for Hyperpyrexia\n1. Malignant hyperthermia\n2. Sepsis - consider intravenous contaminant\n3. Overheating - warming blanket, lights, etc.\n4. Hypothalamic injury\n5. Thyrotoxicosis\n6. Faulty temperature measurement\n7. Osteogenesis imperfecta\n8. Transfusion reaction\n9. Drug reactions: neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), MAO inhibitors, amphetamine, cocaine, tricyclics, atropine, glycopyrrolate, droperidol, metoclopramide, levodopa withdrawal, cogentin, ketamine, EtOH withdrawal, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)\n\nMasseter Spasm and MH\n1. Masseter spasm occurs in as many as 1% of children receiving halothane and succinylcholine.\n2. Masseter spasm is often, but not always seen following succinylcholine administration in patients that develop MH.\n3. Masseter spasm could be considered an early sign of possible MH crisis, especially if other muscle rigidity is present.\n4. Muscle rigidity may develop more slowly during anesthetic if succinylcholine is not used.\n\nTreatment of MH Crisis\n1. Stop inhalation anesthesia\n2. Hyperventilate with 100 % oxygen\n3. Administer dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg as quickly as possible. Continue to give dantrolene until all signs of MH have resolved. This will decrease the incidence of recrudescence. A total dose of 20 mg/kg may be necessary.\n4. Administer cold intravenous fluids - use normal saline, not ringer's lactate\n5. Follow arterial blood gas, electrolytes and glucose. Central venous gases are also sensitive in identifying tissue hypoxia and hypercarbia.\n6. Do not administer calcium channel blockers. They can cause cardiac arrest and hyperkalemia in the presence of dantrolene.\n7. Monitor for relapse\n8. Call the MH Hotline for assistance: 1-800-MH-HYPER\n\nManagement of the MH Susceptible Patient\n1. Avoid triggering agents: succinylcholine and potent inhalation agents\n2. Pretreatment with dantrolene is controversial and usually not indicated\n3. Use cleansed anesthesia machine and fresh circuit. If machine is purged for 20 minutes then it is not necessary to change the CO2 absorbent.\n4. Have MH cart ready with 36 vials of dantrolene imediately available.\n5. MH patients can be done as outpatients as long as appropriate precautions are taken and the patient is monitored in the recovery room for four hours.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 12, 2013\n\n", 
