+    "debriefMsg": "\nThis patient is dehydrated and has restricted filling of her left ventricle due to mitral stenosis.  Recognize that higher left atrial filling pressures due to the stenotic valve to not reflect the ventricular pressure.  Normal appearing pulmonary artery pressure and wedge pressure in this patient are indicative of hypovolemia.  Induction without adequate volume resuscitation will result in severe hypotension and decreased cardiac output.  Tachycardia due to any cause will result in inadequate diastolic filling time for the left ventricle and should therefore be avoided. Loss of the atrial kick due to atrial fibrillation will also result in significantly decreased left ventricular filling.\n\nHypoxia, hypercarbia, acidosis and sympathetic stimulation will result in increased pulmonary vascular resistance and right heart failure.  Certain drugs such as pancuronium, ketamine and nitrous oxide also increase pulmonary vascular resistance.  Vasodilators such as nitroglycerine and sodium nitroprusside as well and inhaled nitric oxide and volatile anesthetics decrease pulmonary vascular resistance.\n\n", 
