+    "learningObj": "\n75 year old female with mitral stenosis (60 kg)\n\nThis patient has a significant cardiac problem with several anesthetic considerations.  Moderate to severe mitral stenosis poses a significant restriction to left ventricular filling and therefore to the cardiac output.  There is also an increase in the left atrial volume and pressure which causes left atrial dilation, predisposing the left atria thrombosis.  The increased left atrial pressure is transmitted to the pulmonary circulation and can cause irreversible increases in pulmonary vascular resistance if chronic, predisposing the right ventricular failure.  She may have significant hypovolemia due to the bowel obstruction. She also requires rapid sequence induction.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Discuss the anesthetic implications of pulmonary hypertension.\n2.  Discuss hemodynamic methods to maintain forward flow in patients with mitral stenosis.\n\nAnesthetic Considerations:\nMitral stenosis\nPulmonary hypertension\nHypovolemia\nFull stomach (bowel obstruction) - prevention of aspiration\n\nSuggested Management:\n1) Routine monitors, with arterial line, central venous catheter, pulmonary artery catheter and urine output monitor.\n2) Adequate volume resuscitation with care to avoid pulmonary congestion and edema.  Recognize that beta blockers mask the tachycardic response to hypovolemia.\n3) Mild sedation to avoid anxiety induced tachycardia with care to avoid oversedation and respiratory depression.\n4) Adequate preoxygenation.\n5) Rapid sequence induction and intubation in view of bowel obstruction and aspiration risk\n6) Maintainance of anesthesia with oxygen, narcotics and inhaled agent as tolerated\n7) Avoid tachycardia to allow adequate diastolic filling time\n8) Recognition and prompt intervention in case of tachycardia and non-sinus rhythm\n9) Adequate ventilation to avoid hypoxia and hypercarbia\n\nCase author: Aparna Ananth, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: March 29, 2013\n\n", 
