+    "preopMsg": "\n75 year old female with moderate mitral stenosis and pulmonary hypertension for exploratory laparotomy\n\nHistory of Present Illness\nPatient complains of having abdominal pain and distension for 2 days associated with nausea and vomiting.  She has had nothing to eat or drink since the onset of symptoms.  She also complains of paroxysmal palpitation in the last 24 hours with associated dizziness.\n\nPast Medical History\nIllnesses: Mitral stenosis from rheumatic heart disease with multiple hospital admissions for congestive heart failure.  Last admission was six months ago.\nSurgeries: Cataract extractions bilaterally 3 years ago, appendectomy in childhood and a C-section over 50 years ago. No anesthetic complications.\n\nMedications:\nAtenolol, Furosemide\n\nFamily History:\nNo anesthetic complications\n\nPhysical Exam:\nThin, elderly female in moderate distress.  Weight: 60kg.  Height: 157 cm.HR: 75/min  BP: 102/65  RR: 22, shallow\nAirway: Mallampatti class I, good neck extension, edentulaous\nLungs: Clear\nHeart: S1S2, opening snap with mid-diastolic rumbling murmur best heard over the apex\n\nLabs:\nHct 45%\nNa-148, K-3.6, Cl-110, HCO3-20, BUN-28, Cr-1.6\n\nEKG: Sinus rhythm at 75/min.  Bifid P waves in lead II\n\nCXR: Cardiomegaly.  Straightening of the left heart border.  Carinal flattening.  Mild pulmonary congestion.\n\nEchocardiography (6 months ago): EF 45%.  Moderate mitral stenosis with valve area of 1.2 cm2.  PASP 48 mmHg\n\nNPO Status:\nPatient has had nothing to eat or drink for 2 days\n\nPatient and Surgeon Requests\nThe surgeon states the procedure will take approximately 3 hours.\n\n", 
