diff --git a/scripts/AboutAnesthSim6.js b/scripts/AboutAnesthSim6.js
index b3b1906..7b19bd9 100644
--- a/scripts/AboutAnesthSim6.js
+++ b/scripts/AboutAnesthSim6.js
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 AboutAnesthSim6 = {
-	"aboutinfo": "\rAbout Anesoft Anesthesia Simulator 6\r\rAnesoft Anesthesia Simulator 6 is an app that provides the opportunity to review the management of anesthetic emergencies and complex cases. Thirty-four different simulated patients are presented and you must manage the airway, ventilation, fluids and medications.  Many emergency situations will occur, requiring rapid diagnosis and proper treatment to avoid disaster.  An automated help system is continuously available during the simulated clinical scenario while a record-keeping system provides a detailed log of your management.\r\r\rContinuing Education Credits\rYou can earn AMA PRA Category 2 credits with Anesthesia Simulator 6. Each physician should claim only those hours that he/she actually spent in the educational activity. Typically 16 credits are claimed for this activity (one half credit per case). Please email a request to jill@anesoft.com if you would like us to send you a CME certificate.\r\r\rExample Anesthesia Simulator Case Simulation\r1. The simulator initially presents the preoperative evaluation, patient image and vital signs monitor.\r\r2. Take a few minutes to familiarize yourself with the main simulator screen. The simulation clock is located in the upper left hand corner of the display. You can click on the clock to pause the simulation.\r\r3. Examine the patient with the Patient menu. You can also use the Patient menu to control the patient position and temperature.\r\r4. Use the Monitor menu to control monitors for the case.  Routine monitors, including ECG, non-invasive blood pressure (NBP), and pulse oximeter are automatically placed. You can also use invasive arterial pressure, a pulmonary artery catheter and a urimeter as required by the clinical situation.\r\r5. Start IV fluids by selecting the Fluids menu and then Adjust IV Fluids. You can run up to two intravenous lines. Select the type of fluid in the content field and adjust the infusion rate. You can also administer a fluid bolus if desired.\r\r6. Select the Airway options to place an anesthesia mask on the patient. Control the oxygen flow using the flowmeter on the anesthesia machine. You are now pre-oxygenating the patient. Notice that the capnogram is activated when the patient has a mask or tube in place.\r\r7. Sedate the patient prior to intubation. If you can't remember the patient's weight, you will find it in the Physical Exam section of the Preop Evalaluation.  \r\r8. Titrate Fentanyl and Propofol to provide a smooth induction. To administer Fentanyl, select it from the Drugs list and administer a bolus of 100 mcg. Select Confirm to give the drug. \r\r10. You may want to administer additional Fentanyl. Then administer Propofol or Etomidate, and Succinylcholine 80 mg. The Drug Info button provides information about the selected drug. \r\r11. When the patient is apneic, control the ventilation by selecting Breathing the Bag Assist/Control. \r\r12. Intubate the patient by selecting Airway then Laryngoscopy.  Resume ventilation with the Breathing menu and confirm correct placement of the endotracheal tube by listening to the breath sounds in the Patient menu, and observing the CO2 wave form.\r\r13. For maintenance anesthesia you can use inhalation agents or continuous infusions. Select inhalation agents on the anesthesia machine in the lower right-hand of the display.  Select intravenous agents from the drug list in the Drugs menu.\r\r14. If you are ever unsure what to do next during the simulation, select  Help > What Next.  Help > What Next provides a brief synopsis of what to do next. \r\r15. You just performed an anesthetic induction. In simulated cases that have a critical incident the emergency is typically programmed to occur a few minutes after induction or incision.\r\r16. You can review the case record using the Case Log option.  You can also Save the record. \r\r17. Learning objectives and suggested management plan are provided for each case in the Case Help > Learning Objectives option.\r\r18. In these simulations it is not necessary to have the surgeon complete the entire operation. A heart icon will appear when you have successfully completed the learning objectives for the simulated case. A ghost indicates the patient has died and you should try the case again. A broken heart means the patient survived but you could have managed the case better. \r\r19. Select New Case to start another simulation. Before you exit the current case you may want to save the record of your case management.\r\r20. You can earn AMA PRA Category 2 credits with Anesthesia Simulator 6. Each physician should claim only those hours that he/she actually spent in the educational activity. Typically 16 credits (one half credit per case) are claimed for this activity. Please email a request to Anesoft Corporation if you would like us to send you a CME certificate.\r\r\rFurther Information:\rAnesoft Corporation\r18606 NW Cervinia Court\rIssaquah, WA 98027 USA\r\rPhone: 425-643-9388\rFAX: 425-643-0092\r\re-mail: jill@anesoft.com\rWebsite: www.anesoft.com\r\r\rWarning\rCustomer acknowledges that the Software is intended as an educational tool for licensed medical professionals and that the Software may not be relied upon in any way in dispensing medical advice or medical treatment.  Anesoft does not warrant the currency or accuracy of the information contained in the Software or that it is free from errors.  It is Customer's responsibility to ensure the accuracy of medical information prior to prescribing medical treatment or otherwise using such information.  \r\r"
+	"aboutinfo": "\rWIP"
 }
